            <div xml:id="d847">

                <pb facs="0994" n="876" sameAs="frus:frus1928v02/pg876"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">462.00 R 296/2524: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Secretary of State to the Chargé in France</hi>
                        (<hi rendition="#italic">Armour</hi>)</head>
                <opener><seg rendition="#center">[Paraphrase]</seg></opener>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1928-12-08T19:00:00-05:00"><hi rendition="#italic">December 8,
                                1928—7 p.m.</hi></date></dateline></opener>
                <p>420. Reparation 66. Reference to your 398, December 8, 11 a.m.<anchor n="18"
                        xml:id="d847-f1"/> I stated in my telegram 416, of December 7, 5 p.m., that
                    no suggestion has been or will be made by the United States as to the role to be
                    played by the Reparation Commission or the Governments in constituting the
                    committee. Furthermore, I had no intention of indicating <pb facs="0995" n="877"
                        xml:id="pg877"/> that the Allied European Governments alone, excluding
                    Germany, should make this request. Neither side nor any individual Government,
                    of course, should use the question of participation of American experts as a
                    weapon.</p>
                <p>Please inform Gilbert, and repeat the above as Department’s No. 124 to
                    Berlin.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Kellogg</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
